<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Rationale (kept in sync with docs/privacy.html — see item 1.3 of the
  2026-07-08 remediation plan; docs/privacy.html's "Cloud backup
  (optional)" section, added the same lot, describes this same set of
  tables and the anonymous user_id in learner-facing language). Updated
  for cloud-sync lot 3 (Sign in with Apple): docs/privacy.html's "Sign in
  with Apple (optional)" bullet, in that same "Cloud backup (optional)"
  section, is where the NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeEmailAddress entry below
  is explained to the learner:

  - Companion chat messages (CompanionChatMessage, via CompanionChatRepository)
    are persisted locally on-device via SwiftData. That local storage alone is
    not third-party data collection.
  - If — and only if — the user configures their own API key for an AI
    provider (Gemini, Groq, Cerebras, OpenRouter, GitHub Models, or Claude;
    see AIRouterService), the text of the conversation or mnemonic request is
    sent to that provider to generate a response. This is opt-in: with no key
    configured, nothing is sent anywhere. Declared below as
    NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUserContent, NOT linked to identity: the
    request carries no Supabase user_id or any other stable identifier, and
    (see cloud-sync bullets below) the sync path deliberately never sends
    this content to Supabase either — the two data flows never combine it
    with an identifier.

  Cloud backup (Supabase, opt-in — see CloudSyncCoordinator.syncNow(), which
  only runs after the learner turns sync on): the app signs in anonymously
  (AnonymousIdentityManager → POST /auth/v1/signup, no name/email/password
  collected at sign-in) and pushes a fixed set of tables, each row stamped
  server-side with that session's user_id via RLS default (auth.uid()) — see
  SyncPayloadBuilder.swift for the exact fields per table. Because every
  pushed row is keyed to the same stable anonymous user_id, and that user_id
  is itself round-tripped back to the device to keep pushing to the same
  rows across app launches, everything pushed here is Linked, not just the
  identifier:

  - NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID: the Supabase user_id that
    AnonymousIdentityManager mints and persists in this device's Keychain.
    It is anonymous (no signup form, no email) but it is an assigned
    account-level identifier used server-side to look up this user's rows
    (RLS: user_id = auth.uid()), which is Apple's own definition of a User
    ID rather than a Device ID. Linked: true (it IS the link).
  - NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeName: UserProfile.displayName, the profile
    name the learner typed in Settings, pushed verbatim in the `profiles`
    row's payload (see SyncPayloadBuilder.row(for: UserProfile)). Linked:
    true — pushed under the same user_id as every other row.
  - EMAIL: deliberately NOT declared, and this is now a measurement rather
    than an assumption. Signing in with Apple (lot 3, AppleSignInFlow.swift)
    requests NO scopes at all — `request.requestedScopes = []`, with even
    `.fullName` deliberately omitted — and no line of Swift in this repo
    reads, stores, or transmits an email string.
    What was unknown until 2026-08-14 was GoTrue's own behaviour on the far
    side of the exchange. Settled by signing in on a real device and
    querying the live project:

        auth.users.email                                  -> NULL
        auth.identities.identity_data->>'email'           -> NULL
        auth.identities.provider                          -> 'apple'

    No address is stored, not even a private relay one; the identity is
    keyed solely on Apple's opaque `sub`. An earlier draft of this file
    declared NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeEmailAddress as a conservative
    guess. That was wrong to keep: an App Store nutrition label claiming
    "email address, linked to you" on an app that collects none is a false
    declaration, not a cautious one. Re-add it ONLY if a future change
    requests the `.email` scope, and re-run the two queries above to
    confirm rather than assuming.
  - NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeProductInteraction: learning progress and
    exercise results — RPGState (xp, level, streaks, sessions/reviews
    completed), Card and VocabularyEntry spaced-repetition state (FSRS
    state, ease, interval, due date, lapse count), ReviewLog (grade,
    response time, the learner's answered value, exercise type/surface),
    VocabularyEncounter (source only — see below),
    ExerciseOutcomeLog (skill, accuracy), and ProfileSettings — the
    learner's own settings, carried inside the `profiles` payload (see
    SyncPayloadBuilder's ProfilePayload.settings): desiredRetention, the
    daily new-card/review limits, and the reminder schedule
    (reviewReminderHour, weeklyCheckInDay, weeklyCheckInHour). Those last
    three are worth naming explicitly rather than hiding under
    "preferences": a reminder hour and a weekly check-in slot say
    something about when the learner is usually available, which is more
    than a UI preference. This is the closest Apple category to "saved
    progress in an app" (one of Apple's own examples under Product
    Interaction). Linked: true — every one of these tables is pushed with
    a row tied to the session's user_id.
  - contextSnippet on VocabularyEncounter (free-form sentence text, often
    Sakura conversation) and the entire companion_chat_messages table are
    deliberately NEVER pushed — see the comments at those call sites in
    SyncPayloadBuilder.swift and CloudSyncCoordinator.swift. Nothing here
    declares them because nothing sends them.

  NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes below covers UserDefaults access (reason
  CA92.1: reading/writing app settings). Network requests to AI providers
  and to Supabase are ordinary URLSession calls and are not part of
  Apple's Required Reason API categories, so no additional entry is
  needed there.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUserContent</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeName</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeProductInteraction</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array><string>CA92.1</string></array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
